<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    ===================================================================
    THE ONLY PROJECT IN THIS SOLUTION WITH NO CONTROLLERS.
    ===================================================================

    It was created with `dotnet new web`, the EMPTY ASP.NET Core template,
    rather than the controller-based webapi template every service used. There
    is no Controllers folder, no DTO, no DbContext, no domain, and no test
    project, because there is nothing here to test: the gateway's entire
    behaviour is configuration.

    (Incidentally, the reason this sentence avoids writing that template's
    flag out in full is that an XML comment may not contain two consecutive
    hyphens. It is a genuinely surprising build error the first time you hit
    it, so: now you have hit it.)

    That is the rule for a gateway, and it is the one people break first:

      IT ROUTES. IT DOES NOT DECIDE.

    The moment a gateway starts stitching two services' responses together,
    enriching a payload, or checking a business rule, it becomes a service that
    every team has to deploy through - a distributed monolith with a single
    point of contention. Every "just this once" is how that happens.

    What legitimately belongs here is CROSS-CUTTING EDGE CONCERNS, the things
    that are true of every request regardless of which service serves it:
    routing, rate limiting, TLS termination, coarse authentication, request
    logging and correlation ids. Not business.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- YARP: Microsoft's reverse proxy, configured from appsettings.json. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Yarp.ReverseProxy" Version="2.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The gateway validates tokens too - the same way every service does, with
      the same shared library. That is not redundancy, it is defence in depth:
      the edge rejects obviously-unauthenticated traffic cheaply, and each
      service still checks for itself because a gateway is not a security
      boundary you want to be the ONLY one.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BuildingBlocks\CommerceFlow.BuildingBlocks.Authentication\CommerceFlow.BuildingBlocks.Authentication.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
